          "gov.dwp.universal_credit.standard_allowance.amount.SINGLE_YOUNG",
          "gov.hmrc.council_tax.high_value_surcharge.amount[1].threshold"
        ],
        "reason": "The installed benefit uprating index follows lagged calendar-year CPI: the parameters uprated by it, the UC standard allowance among them, step by 2.3% in April 2027 against September 2026 CPI of 3.5% (ONS D7G7, published 21 October 2026). The UC protected health element's 2027 amount also reads the September 2026 input, which is still a forecast, and so do the triple lock's CPI leg and the September CPI index, by which additional State Pension and new State Pension protected payments rise once policyengine-uk #2336 is in the model. The 2026-27 rates are hard-coded and unaffected. policyengine-uk #2533 must both enter the September outturn and repoint the benefit index to September CPI. Until both steps are in the model, measures touching these benefit levels from 2027 are held at provisional."
      },
      {
        "key": "vat_consumption_coverage",
        "parameters": [
          "variable:vat",
          "variable:vat_change"
        ],
        "reason": "The installed model grosses household VAT up to receipts by a single factor of 0.38 (gov.simulation.microdata_vat_coverage). policyengine-uk #2187 divides instead by survey consumption coverage times the household share of VAT receipts, 0.67 \u00d7 0.7 = 0.469, so this model's VAT is about 23% higher (the UK hub's notice 59 puts it about 24% high on Microcosm), and a measure that moves a VAT rate, the VAT base or the consumption VAT is charged on can be overstated by as much. A measure built with the VAT base template is held too, though the term the template adds is not grossed. #2187 is not in this model. Until a pair with it is adopted, such measures are held at provisional."
      }
